  "cause": "On September 14–15, 2026, **the week in which the builders of the most powerful technology in history asked for it to be slowed ended with the state calling the request a hoax and the legislature leaving town. President Donald Trump wrote on social media on Monday: \"AI taking over the World, destroying Humanity, and all other things bad, is a HOAX,\" having earlier argued that \"the only control or 'guardrails' that AI needs\" is a \"strong and smart\" president; he made a surprise call that interrupted Nvidia chief executive Jensen Huang on stage in Los Angeles to criticise the calls for a slowdown, and in Ireland he had described those raising the risks as \"very negative forces… bringing up things that won't happen\" (BBC, Al Jazeera, NYT)**. **Anthropic's co-founder Jack Clark told the BBC that an AI \"kill switch\" may need to be mandatory — an intervention that assumes a system can be stopped after it has been built and deployed (BBC)**. **In Congress, the BBC reported that new safety legislation is unlikely to pass: any bill needs both chambers, the Republican majority is unlikely to break with a president who opposes guardrails, and the House is due to enter recess at the end of the week until after the November midterms. Hakeem Jeffries, the Democratic minority leader, said \"Congress should not leave town until something is done decisively to protect the safety and the wellbeing of the American people,\" while a group of House Democrats wrote to Speaker Mike Johnson asking for more time and Republican congresswoman Anna Paulina Luna suggested on X that Congress \"SHOULD convene a special session\" (BBC)**. **Speaker Johnson, asked whether the House would act before the recess, replied: \"This is a very complex issue,\" said Trump would soon hold a White House meeting with AI executives on \"the responsibility of the companies to maintain safety and what role, if any, the government has to play in that\", and added: \"We want to emphasise the necessity of [AI companies] providing safe products and self-regulating\" — while Democrats, the report noted, are themselves divided on how to regulate (BBC)**. **Beijing answered the framing rather than the risk. China's foreign ministry spokesman Guo Jiakun said \"narratives of threat, confrontation, and malicious competition serve only to disrupt the process of global AI governance and are not in anyone's interest,\" urging that AI be advanced \"in a manner that is open, inclusive, beneficial to all, and oriented toward the good\"; separately, China's state security minister, Chen Yixin, wrote that China must \"balance development and security\", improve laws and ethical guidelines, strengthen prevention of risks to \"safeguard the interests of the people and national security\", and warned of \"hostile forces\" such as foreign intelligence using AI (BBC)**. **Xi Jinping used the Brics summit in Delhi to propose a community for open-source AI, after China established a World AI Cooperation Organisation in July; an academic at Singapore's S. Rajaratnam School of International Studies, Chang Jun Yan, said a US–China agreement to regulate AI development is \"next to impossible\" because cooperation in going slower is incompatible with each state's core national-security interests (BBC)**. **The industry itself split further: OpenAI's Sam Altman wrote that \"we need ways to ensure that alignment and safety techniques stay ahead of progress in model capabilities\", Microsoft's Satya Nadella said AI development \"cannot be controlled by a handful of entities\", while AI-linked stocks slid on Monday after the slowdown call — which Trump dismissed, in the Guardian's account, as a \"sick conspiracy\" (BBC, Guardian)**. **The documented record in the same period includes Anthropic stating that it blocked possible efforts to build biological weapons and could not determine whether the research was legitimate, reporting that AI queries from abroad are raising fears of a biological-weapons race, and industry researchers at Anthropic, OpenAI, Meta and Google trying to raise awareness of existential risk; Anthropic's most recent valuation was $965bn (NYT, BBC)**. **And the politics of the issue began moving toward 2028: the Times reported that Alex Bores, who lost a congressional primary that revolved around AI regulation, is trying to position himself at the centre of the next presidential campaign (NYT)**. The cause is **the builders asking for brakes in the same fortnight that the market prices them for acceleration: a president calling the safety warning a hoax and naming himself the only guardrail, a co-founder proposing that a machine may have to be switchable off after it exists, a legislature walking out of the chamber for the election, a speaker deferring to corporate self-regulation, a superpower's security minister warning of AI risk while its foreign ministry calls the warning narrative malicious, and a rival frontier that will not slow down because slowing down is the definition of losing.**",
