  "verse": "With regard to both the creational laws and recommendations as well as your human laws and ordinances you are free in deciding whether you want to follow them or disregard them, in which case you are also conscious — at least with regard to your earthly-human laws and ordinances etc. — that the cause of a breach of the law or ordinance will entail the effect of you being called to account.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 28, Verse 242",
  "hinge": "The cause is the **choice to govern by decree in a country whose law still answers back — a facility built by executive order in eight days and staffed by a state while the federal government pays, an order changing how a third of the country votes and a court that declines to let it, rules set to take effect on dates chosen so that they apply before they are tested, and an election prepared with watchers trained to disbelieve the count.** Verse 242 sets out the accounting that applies to all of it: \"the cause of a breach of the law or ordinance will entail the effect of you being called to account.\" Three institutions called the administration to account in a single week — and the account is not settled by winning the injunction or losing it; the reckoning is cumulative in the people who were held in an outdoor cage, the students whose status depends on a filing, and the voters whose rules changed and changed back within a month. That same law permits other causes, and each is a document that could be written this week: an inspection finding with a consequence attached, statutory criteria enacted rather than litigated, enforceable minimum standards for detention, and election rules agreed by both sides before the votes are counted. Whether this becomes the autumn the country settles its rules or the autumn it argues about them after the fact, is being decided in choices available now — and the effect will follow the cause that is set."
